# fixedbugs, defect 560, lane b18-ffi, 2026-10-10 (panel 202's R3; the
# spec-warden's `cfg`).
#
# One module's header defines `LIMIT` as 100, and another's caps at `LIMIT`,
# defining it as 10 where no header read before it has. `build` reads each in
# its own module's unit, so `cap` caps at 10, and prints 3 and 10. Until
# 2026-10-10 `heroes test` read both in one unit, the first module's first,
# and failed the correct test below, *left: 50 right: 10*.
#
# The modules' names are chosen for the key the compiler walks `use` lines
# by (panel 238's R4, `selfhost/use_key.hero`, whose test names them):
# `limit_half` before `then_cap_ten`, the order in which the defect shows.
# Under the key the order written reaches no walk, so a module named to sort
# first would make this a case that passes on the compiler it was written
# against (the sitting's compiler-engineer ran this text on the compiler of
# `55d20a362`, from before the repair, and it failed, as it should).
use fixedbugs560/limit_half
use fixedbugs560/then_cap_ten

function main()
    print(limit_half.halved(x: 6))
    print(then_cap_ten.capped(v: 50))

test "cap is ten"
    assert then_cap_ten.capped(v: 50) == 10
